<<<
convenient novelties, (1) those that | must sound the e are unobjectionable, animatedly, offendedly,

e.g. | unstintedly; (2) of those in which the e can (physically) be either sounded or silent none (with the exception of the classes in N° 3 & 4 below) are tolerable unless the | writer is prepared to have the e sounded; thus the user of composedly, confusedly, dispersedly, absorbedly, & declaredly, will not resent _ their being given four syllables each, & they pass the test; but no-one
===
convenient novelties, (1) those that *must* sound the e are unobjectionable, e.g. *animatedly, offendedly, unstintedly*; (2) of those in which the e can (physically) be either sounded or silent none (with the exception of the classes in N°s 3 & 4 below) are tolerable unless the writer is prepared to have the e sounded; thus the user of *composedly, confusedly, dispersedly, absorbedly, & declaredly,* will not resent their being given four syllables each, & they pass the test; but no-one
>>>

<<<
+ will write experiencedly, accomplishedly, boredly, skilledly, or discouragedly, & consent to the ed’s being a distinct syllable; they are therefore ruled out; (3) hurriedly suggests that such forms as palsiedly, worrtedly, variedly, frenziedly, & studiedly (from verbs in unaccented -ğ) are legitimate; (4) words in unaccented -wre, -owr, or -er, seem to form passable adverbs
===
+ will write *experiencedly, accomplishedly, boredly, skilledly,* or *discouragedly,* & consent to the *ed*'s being a distinct syllable; they are therefore ruled out; (3) *hurriedly* suggests that such forms as *palsiedly, worriedly, variedly, frenziedly,* & *studiedly* (from verbs in unaccented -y̆) are legitimate; (4) words in unaccented *-ure, -our,* or *-er,* seem to form passable adverbs
>>>

